\title{Notes on Rubik's abstract polytopes}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{frap}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-10-09}

\p{These are the accompanying mathematical notes for the experimental research
project \href{https://github.com/utensil/frap}{FRAP}, \em{Formalizing Rubik's
Abstract Polytopes}. The notes ask how the mathematics of abstract polytopes,
incidence actions and group constraints can explain the concrete practice of
solving Rubik puzzles. The longer motivation, history and scope are recorded in
[why these notes connect polytopes and puzzles](frap-001E).}

\p{The route is staged so each abstraction earns a concrete consequence. We
begin with incidence and shape symmetry, apply local facet moves to a
tetrahedron and a cube, then pass to layered cubes, orientations and
reachability. The later notes return to human methods, computer search,
replay and play. [How the notes are organized](frap-000C) is the working route
through those chapters; the companion repository is still an early research
project, so formalization and executable solver work remain part of the scope.}

